 eat  that food and allow yourself to eat it  in a calm relaxing environment I know a  lot of people feel really anxious about  the phrase unconditional permission to  eat and I understand why I mean like  there is no condition or situation where  you would say no to a cupcake but if you  find that you're binging on certain  foods and have a hard time stopping this  may be a sign that you have been  restricting and it's really not working  out for you now I want you to take a  second to think for a moment about the  foods that you truly truly enjoy not the  foods that society has told you that you  should enjoy but the foods that bring a  smile to your face and just like make  you feel really good after eating them  in a lot of cases people really struggle  to answer this question I mean for so  long we're told that we should eat this  we shouldn't eat that and that our food  choices are made  based on the suggestions or pressures of  others so if you're having a really hard  time deciding what foods you genuinely  like I suggest you take a week or so to  test out different foods and flavors  find out what tastes you really like and  what tastes maybe you don't like foods  that make you feel good and those that  make you feel kind of crappy now once  you find out what foods you actually  really like and allow yourself to eat  these foods you'll likely eventually  find yourself eating an amount that  you're actually hungry for because after  that it's not enjoyable and it doesn't  feel or even taste that good when we eat  the foods that we enjoy we don't spend  time eating foods just trying to fill a  void and ultimately we're able to find  satisfaction with the right amount of  food for us in that moment now step 2 is  discover the pleasure of food so if  you're really struggling with finding  what foods you enjoy this step is super  important as you experiment with  different kinds of foods consider the  sensual qualities of food so taste is  the food sweet salty bitter sour do you  enjoy it when do you enjoy it at  breakfast lunch dinner do these things  change throughout the day figuring out  what flavors you enjoy and when you  enjoy them can really help you discover  if a food will satisfy you then there's  texture do you like crunchy foods soft  foods creamy foods does this change  depending on the time of day or the food  can certain textures be more or less  appealing at different times so for  example the idea of eating a crunchy  cabbage salad at 7:00 a.m.  may not be so appetizing but a bowl of  creamy soft oatmeal might be then  there's aroma so many people do not know  this but digestion actually starts  before food even hits our tongue the  smell of food can stimulate the release  of different enzymes that get our mouths  to salivate so for example the smell of  roasted coffee beans in the morning may  rev up your appetite but the smell of  roast beef might not if the smell of the  food is enjoyable for you in the moment  it's likely going to lead to  satisfaction when you eat it  next is appearance I mean this one is he  for people we like food that looks  appetizing  in fact dishes that have more colors  going on tend to be viewed as more  appealing than plates that have very  bland or subdued colors so if you have a  salad with some green lettuce green  peppers and green broccoli it's probably  not gonna look that good but if you had  a salad with some green lettuce red  peppers purple onions and orange carrots  that will probably be way more  interesting and appealing the more  exciting a meal looks ultimately the  more enjoyment we're going to get out of  it this was really one of my main goals  with my book the mindful Glo cookbook  and if you look at the images you'll see  that I am obsessed with color I believe  that if it looks good it's more likely  to be exciting and you're more able to  derive a greater amount of pleasure from  eating it also think about temperature  so time of day mood the weather outside  and other factors can really change how  appealing a certain food temperature is  if it's freezing outside you're probably  going to want something warm and cozy  that's gonna be a little bit more  enjoyable if it's the middle of summer  you'll probably find a hot chocolate not  as appetizing so finding what  temperature of food you enjoy can help  you experience eating even better so do  you like your dinner piping hot or do  you like to give it a few minutes to  cool it down do you like your drinks  with lots of ice or to prefer it closer  to room temperature find what works for  you to make your meals more satisfying  and enjoyable and let's talk about  volume for a second  so some foods are categorized as being  heavy and filling while others are  thought to be more light and airy some  days you may just want foods that are  more filling like a big pasta dinner  while other days you might want a  lighter meal like a salad or soup in  either case it's really about listening  to what your body truly wants even if  these foods are normally tasty to you  you may not find them very appealing if  your stomach is upset or you're already  full so truly every day every meal can  look different I want you to keep in  mind that what one person finds  appetizing  not the appetizing for you or vice-versa  this is totally normal and okay we don't  want to necessarily be yakking somebody  else's young but the amazing thing about  humans is that we're all so different so  find what makes you happy and satisfied  and go with that now when you're  thinking about what you want to eat take  these sensory properties of food into  consideration so think to yourself what  do I want to eat what smells good right  now  what looks really good right now how  would XY and Z food taste at this very  moment and do I want something sweet or  salty or sour what kind of texture of  food do I want am I in the mood for  something hot cold or maybe something in  between more room temperature also how's  my stomach volume right now what a heavy  or light meal feel better  asking these questions can really help  you get to the bottom of what you're  truly wanting rather than just eating  food arbitrarily in hopes that you  figure it out step 3 make eating a more  enjoyable experience if you've ever  visited certain countries in Europe  you'll notice that mealtime is so much  more value to compare to meals here in  North America in some cases businesses  totally just closed down at lunch time  people meet up with friends and family  and most take a lot longer than 20  minutes to eat their lunch in comparison  North Americans tend to eat their lunch  at their desk in front of a TV or their  computer or in their car while driving  if lucky you may have five minutes of  peace to eat your lunch  uninterrupted whatever it is this is  really a very enjoyable experience when  we rush to eat our meals we don't give  ourselves an opportunity to stop and  actually enjoy the experience of eating  think back to a time that you had a nice  family dinner that lasted upwards of an  hour or two you sat down you ate your  food slowly you enjoyed your  conversation sipped your wine and really  experienced eating as it was meant to be  I don't know about you but I rarely get  to experience food like this anymore our  lives are so fast-paced  that we really have lost the sanctity of  eating and enjoying food in exchange for  efficiency and productivity so to work  on intuitive eating I want you to take  time to savor your food and the  experience of eating this doesn't have  to happen at every meal but try to use  some of these tips at one meal at least  every single day so these tips are  dedicate time to eating and eating only  so give yourself a time allowance to  focus only on the active eating even if  it's only 10 or 15 minutes this is way  better than what we normally allow  ourselves to sit down at the table so  eating in front of the television in  your car or even in the kitchen counter  does not set you up for a  distraction-free meal by sitting down at  the table this is really a visual and  physical cue that you're about to eat  and that can increase attention and  overall satisfaction 3 practice deep  breathing so meditation and breathing  focus practices can help calm yourself  down before a meal this increases your  focus on food and away from distractions  which can help you eat slower and more  intuitively that brings me to tip 4 slow  down now remember that the slower you  eat the more flavors and textures you'll  notice this act can really help you get  in tune with the pleasurable aspects of  food and helps you notice as your hunger  levels to gradually decrease one little  tip is to put down your fork and knife  periodically and that can kind of signal  to you to maybe check in with your  hunger and how pleasurable the eating  experience is next don't wait until your  ravenous to eat if you wait until you're  starving to start a meal you're less  likely to enjoy the foods in front of  you because you're so focused on  satisfying your intense hunger in that  moment most people find the most  pleasure in food when they're moderately  hungry so refer back to the hunger scale  that we talked about last week and try  not to let yourself get down to a one or  two too often next avoid tension at  meals I know this is easier said than  done but your eating experience will  definitely suffer if you're focused on  an argument with your friend partner or  family member  let's keep mealtime a sacred fight free  also provide variety lack of food  choices creates a sense of deprivation  and in turn you may end up dedicating a  lot of time to searching for something  to eat try to allow all types of food in  your fridge and cupboards because that  will help you find what you're looking  for when that craving hits and again  over time you'll notice that you won't  always be hunting for cookies or chips  that that doesn't necessarily mean  that's what you're craving but rather  what your body truly wants in that  moment now if you're having a hard time  remembering all this level and trebly  analyse rush give a great and easy way  to remember it they call it the 3 s's  eat slowly eat sensually and savor every  bite another thing to keep in mind is  the environment that you're in is your  kitchen or dining room really messy is  it dark and unwelcoming are you eating  in your car on the go at your work desk  etc ideally you want to eat a room that  feels welcoming with limited  distractions so a good tip is to make  your dining table only for dining  rather than table for multiple tasks  this will limit the amount of chaos you  associate with this environment and  really allows you to focus down on  eating now let's get to step 4 do not  settle now when it comes to a treat of  eating you are in charge if you do enjoy  the food that you're eating you're more  than welcome to keep eating it to your  liking and you also don't have to finish  eating an entire plate of food if you  don't want to you can enjoy food in the  moment and then lose interest as you  take a few more bites  that's the great thing about intuitive  eating there's really no concrete rules  with how much or her little food you  need to eat it's really all up to your  hunger and your preferences so for  example if you're having dinner and you  find that the potatoes are undercooked  then you don't need to feel pressured to  eat all of them just because they're on  your plate instead focus on enjoying the  other foods at the table maybe the  cooked vegetables or the rice or the  chicken or whatever it is that's been  served also it's okay to enjoy the real  deal for example let's say you really  really really loved a particular brand  of chocolate chip cookies but  in an effort to be healthier you try to  make low sugar chocolate chip cookies at  home in a lot of cases the cookie just  is not going to taste the same and let's  be real and never well you don't have to  settle for the healthier cookie just  because you feel like you should enjoy  the product that you truly like I mean  tell yourself I'm only going to eat that  if I'm really going to enjoy it  which can really help prevent overeating  through the trial and error of finding  something like it step 5 check in with  yourself now while you're eating take a  few seconds to really just like reflect  and check in with yourself is eating  this still enjoyable  am I still finding this really tasty in  a lot of cases the first few bites of  any meal are really the best and and it  does start to dwindle after the tenth or  in some cases the 20th bite now in  principle three I talked about a  phenomenon known as habituation which is  the more a person is exposed to a food  the less appealing that food becomes  that is totally normal  now a good way to check in with yourself  is to really rate your taste pleasure at  the beginning of eating a specific food  and then again after a few bites and  then finally when you're done so one  would be the least pleasurable ten would  be the most pleasurable in most cases  you're gonna notice that your taste  rating goes down the more exposure that  you have to that food if you notice that  you're not enjoying the food anymore  then this might be a really good time to  stop and wait until you're hungry again  for more of that food remember that the  food is always available so why waste  your time eating it when you don't  necessarily find it pleasurable anymore  save it for when it does taste great  leftovers can be a beautiful thing now  there may be situations when you can't  do all of these steps of course and that  is totally okay there are going to be  days and you're busy that you have to  eat in the car and there are going to be  days when there are only certain foods  available and maybe you have to eat them  to satisfy your hunger there are going  to be days when you're having a super  busy day so that you know you need to  eat a little bit more  then you're hungry for because you won't  get another chance for six hours  intuitive eating is not a perfect  process but rather it's a guidance to a  healthier relationship with food and  eating finally I get a lot of comments  from some of you that are watching that  say that their family or friends comment  on their food choices I'm here to give  you 100% permission to reclaim your  right to enjoy eating the foods that you  love you do not need to defend your  eating choices to anyone but if you want  to respond you can kindly remind them  that you welcome any foods that bring  you joy into your life and you really  try to encourage them to do the same I  also have a whole video on food and body  shaming with lots of really fun  responses to a lot of these kinds of  family comments so you can check that  out right here now for your homework  this week I want you to focus on  practicing steps 1 through 5 and take  time to figure out the foods that really  bring you joy and pleasure and try to  dedicate the time to eat your meal  slowly again again not every day will be  perfect but the more you focus on  enjoying your meals the more food will  become a friend rather than an enemy  continue to work on these steps  throughout your intuitive eating journey  and stay tuned for our next video on  principle number 7 cope with your  emotions without using food on that note  I hope you all enjoyed this video if you  did be sure to give it the thumbs up  leave me a comment below if you have any  questions related to intuitive eating  and I may answer them in an upcoming  video don't forget to subscribe to the  channel and I will see you next time on  Abby's kitchen bye  [Music]
 

